\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\title{Algorithm for Generating Array B}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\section*{Problem Statement}
Given an array \( A \) containing \( n \) integers. For a given number \( x \) that appears in \( A \), we define the multiplicity of \( x \) in array \( A \) as the number of occurrences of \( x \) in \( A \). We wish to generate a sorted array \( B \) of size \( n \) that contains the multiplicity of each of the numbers appearing in \( A \).

Propose an algorithm that solves the problem with the best expected time complexity.

\section*{Proposed Algorithm}

1. \textbf{Count Frequencies:} Use a hash map (or dictionary) to count the frequency of each number in array \( A \). This step will take \( O(n) \) time on average.

2. \textbf{Populate Array B:} Iterate over each element in array \( A \) and for each element, look up its frequency in the hash map to populate array \( B \). This step also takes \( O(n) \) time.

3. \textbf{Sort Array B:} Finally, sort array \( B \) to ensure it is in non-decreasing order. Sorting will take \( O(n \log n) \) time.

\section*{Complexity Analysis}
The expected time complexity of the proposed algorithm is dominated by the sorting step, resulting in \( O(n \log n) \) expected time complexity.

\end{document}
```

