```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}

\section*{Two Stacks A,B}

\subsection*{Implementation of Enqueue Operation and its Time Complexity:}
Push to Stack $A$. Running time: $O(1)$.

\subsection*{Implementation of PEnqueue Operation and its Time Complexity:}
Push to Stack $B$. Running time: $O(1)$.

\subsection*{Implementation of Dequeue Operation:}
If Stack $B$ is not empty – perform Pop from it.\\
Otherwise – transfer all elements from Stack $A$ to Stack $B$ one by one, then perform Pop from $B$.

\subsection*{Amortized Time Analysis for the Operation in the Structure:}
Consider a sequence of $N$ operations. In such a sequence, at most $N$ elements have been inserted into the structure (by Enqueue and PEnqueue operations).\\
If an element is inserted via PEnqueue (i.e., into Stack $B$), then at most two operations are performed on it: Push to $B$ and maybe Pop from $B$.\\
If an element is inserted via Enqueue (i.e., into Stack $A$), then at most four operations are performed on it: Push to $A$, and maybe Pop from $A$, Push to $B$, and maybe Pop from $B$.\\
Thus, in any case, $O(1)$ time is spent per element.\\
Therefore, for any sequence of $N$ operations, the running time is $O(N)$. It is clear that the running time is also $O(1)$ per individual operation, because each operation in the sequence takes $O(1)$ time.\\
Hence, every sequence, particularly in the worst-case scenario, takes running time $O(N)$.\\
The amortized time per operation is therefore: $O(1)$.

\end{document}
```

