\end{itemize}

Each set is represented by a tree. Each node in the tree contains an element and a pointer to its parent in the tree. The root of the tree also stores the size of the set. The root serves as the "representative" of the set. For example, the tree

\begin{center}
\{x, y, z, w, u\}, size = 5.
\end{center}

represents the set \(\{x, y, z, w, u\}\) with size 5.

The implementation of \textbf{Makeset} allocates a new node constituting a new tree. At the root, the element \( x \) is stored, along with the size which is 1.

The implementation of \textbf{Union(s1, s2)} is performed by making the root of the smaller set a child of the root of the larger set. Additionally, the size of the root of the larger set is updated to be the sum of the sizes of the two sets.

The implementation of \textbf{Find(z)} is done by advancing from node \( z \) according to the parent pointers until the root is reached and then returning the root. 

Additionally, the \textbf{Delete(z)} operation is added, which deletes the element stored in the node pointed to by \( z \). (Again - as in Find, a pointer directly to \( z \) is received). To support this operation, nodes containing elements marked as deleted are allowed. At the root of a set, the number of deleted elements in the tree is also recorded. Furthermore, in this section, each node will hold a list of pointers to its children, in addition to a pointer to its parent. (In the implementation described in the previous section, there were no pointers to children, only to parents).

The implementation of \textbf{Delete(z)} marks the element in node \( z \) as deleted. Then, it follows the parent pointers to the root, increasing the number of deleted elements in the set by 1. If the number of deleted elements is greater than half the number of elements in the set, the entire tree representing the set is scanned, and any node holding a deleted element is released. Among the non-deleted elements, one is chosen to be the root, and all other non-deleted elements are made its children.

For example, if the set \( s = \{z, u, a, b\} \) is represented by the structure in the following form:

\begin{center}
\textbf{Delete(u)}
\end{center}

then the tree representing the set after the operation will be:

assuming \( a \) is chosen to be the root.

\section*{Time Complexity Analysis}

How long does it take to perform a series of \( m \) operations, where the number of elements at any moment does not exceed \( n \), and where the number of Find or Delete operations is \( k \)? (The remaining \( m-k \) operations are Makeset or Union). Prove your answer.

\end{document}
