```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}

\section*{Question:}

Given a hash function $h$ from a set $U$ to the range $[m]=\{0,1,\ldots,m-1\}$. Assume that $h$ can be computed on any given element of $U$ in $O(1)$ time. Let there be a set $D \subset U$ of size $n$. Describe the most efficient deterministic algorithm possible for finding the number of collisions of $h$ on the elements of $D$. What is the complexity of the algorithm you described?

\section*{Answer:}

To find the number of collisions efficiently, we can use the following algorithm:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initialize an array $count$ of size $m$ with all zeros. The purpose of this array is to store the number of elements in $D$ that hash to each value.
    \item For each element $x \in D$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Compute $h(x)$.
        \item Increment $count[h(x)]$ by 1.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Initialize a variable $collisionCount$ to 0. This will store the number of collisions.
    \item For each value in the $count$ array:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If a value is greater than 1, increment $collisionCount$ by ($\text{value} - 1$). This accounts for the number of pairs of elements that hash to the same position.
    \end{itemize}
    \item Return $collisionCount$.
\end{enumerate}

The algorithm runs in $O(n + m)$ time complexity:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Initializing the array $count$ takes $O(m)$ time.
    \item Computing the hash for each of the $n$ elements and updating the array $count$ takes $O(n)$ time, since each hash computation is $O(1)$.
    \item Calculating the number of collisions from the $count$ array also takes an additional $O(m)$ time.
\end{itemize}

Thus, the overall complexity of the algorithm is $O(n + m)$.

\end{document}
```

