```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}

\section*{Refutation}

We will perform a reduction to the sorting problem. Suppose we can sort an array in less than $O(n \log n)$, which we showed in class is a lower bound. Given an array, we iterate over all its elements and find for each one the insertion position in the tree, and then insert it. It is known that a series of insertions when the insertion position in the WAVL tree is known is $O(n)$ amortized per operation, and therefore for $n$ insertions, we pay $O(n \log \log n)$. After constructing the tree, we traverse it in an in-order walk and output the elements into an array in sorted order $O(n)$. In total, we obtained that the complexity of sorting is $O(n) + O(n) + O(n \log \log n) = O(n \log \log n)$, in contradiction to the lower bound we saw in class.

\end{document}
```

