\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}

\begin{document}

In this question, we will deal with data structures for maintaining lists that support the following operations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $L \leftarrow \text{MakeList}$ - Create an empty list.
    \item $A \leftarrow \text{Insert}(L, x, i)$ - Insert the element $x$ at position $i$ in the list $L$. Return a ListNode containing the newly created element. (The indices of the elements at positions $i$ and onwards increase by 1).
    \item $A \leftarrow \text{Retrieve}(L, i)$ - Return the ListNode containing the element at the $i$-th position in the list.
    \item $L_3 \leftarrow \text{Concat}(L_1, L_2)$ - Concatenate the lists $L_1$ and $L_2$ and return the concatenated list. The lists $L_1$ and $L_2$ cease to exist.
    \item $\text{WhichList}(A)$ - Return the list in which the ListNode $A$ is currently located.
\end{itemize}

You need to describe different implementations of the data structure according to the following requirements: $\text{Concat}(L_1, L_2)$ in $O(1)$ in worst-case. The other operations should be as fast as possible in the worst-case. In all cases, $n$ is the length of the list, or the sum of the lengths of the lists on which the operation is performed. Record the timings of the various operations in your implementations in a table.

\end{document}
```

