```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}
\usepackage{enumitem}

\begin{document}

We wish to implement a data structure for maintaining lists that supports the following operations:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
    \item \texttt{MakeList()} - Create an empty list \(S\).
    \item \texttt{Retrieve(S, i)} - Return the \(i\)-th element of the list \(S\) (indices starting from 0).
    \item \texttt{InsertLast(S, x)} - Add \(x\) to the end of the list \(S\).
    \item \texttt{DeleteLast(S)} - Remove the last element from the list \(S\) (assuming the list is not empty).
\end{enumerate}

We will analyze the amortized cost to implement this structure using an array.

The \texttt{MakeList()} operation allocates an initial array (of length 1000). When the array becomes full, a new array with 100 elements more than its current size is allocated, and the elements are copied to it. Additionally, when there are 200 unused elements at the end of the array, a new array that is shorter by 100 elements than its current size is allocated, and the elements are moved to it.

Now, assume that when the array becomes full, a new array three times its current size is allocated, and the elements are copied to it. Furthermore, when only \(1/9\) of the elements in the array are in use, a new array one-third of its current size is allocated, and the elements are moved to it.

\subsection*{b. What is the amortized complexity of the various operations under this implementation? Assume \(n\) is the number of elements in the list.}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{MakeList()} - The operation of creating a new list initially allocates a fixed amount of space, which can be considered \(O(1)\) in terms of initial setup.
    \item \texttt{Retrieve(S, i)} - Accessing an element by index is \(O(1)\) since it involves a direct index lookup in an array.
    \item \texttt{InsertLast(S, x)} - This operation is generally \(O(1)\) except when a resize is necessary. Using the resizing strategy where the array size is tripled, the amortized cost can be shown to be \(O(1)\) given this geometric growth, which compensates for the resizing cost over a series of insertions.
    \item \texttt{DeleteLast(S)} - Similar to the insert operation, removing an element from the end is \(O(1)\) generally. The cost of resizing down, when fewer elements are used, is also amortized to \(O(1)\) when the array is reduced in size by a constant factor.
\end{itemize}

Thus, with the described resizing strategies of geometric growth and shrinkage, the amortized complexity of both \texttt{InsertLast} and \texttt{DeleteLast} operations is \(O(1)\).

\end{document}
```

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}

\begin{document}

We wish to implement a data structure for maintaining lists that supports the following operations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{MakeList()} - Create an empty list \( S \).
    \item \textbf{Retrieve(\( S \), \( i \))} - Return the \( i \)-th element in list \( S \) (indices start at 0).
    \item \textbf{InsertLast(\( S \), \( x \))} - Add \( x \) to the end of the list \( S \).
    \item \textbf{DeleteLast(\( S \))} - Remove the last element from list \( S \) (assuming the list is not empty).
\end{enumerate}

We will analyze the amortized number of operations required to implement this structure using an array.

In section A: the \textbf{MakeList()} operation allocates an initial array (length 1000). When the array is full, a new array with 100 more elements than the current size is allocated, and the elements are copied to it. Additionally, when there are 200 unused elements at the end of the array, a new array 100 elements shorter than the current size is allocated, and the elements are transferred to it.

In section B: we assume that when the array is full, a new array of size three times the current size is allocated, and the elements are copied to it. Additionally, when only 1/9 of the elements in the array are in use, a new array of size 1/3 of the current size is allocated, and the elements are transferred to it.

Now we add the following operation to the data structure:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Delete(\( S \), \( i \))} - Remove the item at position \( i \) in \( S \). (Assuming there is such an item.) The indices of the elements after \( i \) are decreased by 1.
\end{enumerate}
We'll consider an implementation where deletion is done lazily, i.e., we mark the item as deleted, but do not shift the subsequent elements. In addition to the array, we will maintain a singly linked list that contains all the deleted indices in the array, sorted (i.e., the elements that were removed). When changing the size of the array (increasing or decreasing), we copy the array again, allowing us to truly remove the elements that were just marked as deleted, making the list of deleted indices empty.
Increasing and decreasing the array size is conducted as described in section B.

For example, consider the following sequence of operations:

\begin{verbatim}
S = MakeList()
Insert-Last(S, 15)
Insert-Last(S, 20)
Insert-Last(S, 25)
Insert-Last(S, 30)
Insert-Last(S, 35)
Delete(S, 2)
Delete(S, 0)
Retrieve(S, 0) — returns 20
Delete(S, 1)
Retrieve(S, 1) — returns 35
\end{verbatim}

Here is how the array and the linked list would look at the end of these operations:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Internal array: [NULL, 20, NULL, NULL, 35, \dots]
    \item Deleted indices list: 0 => 2 => 3
\end{itemize}

Note that the length of this list is equal to the number of deletions that have occurred since the last time we changed the size of the array. We denote this value by \( d \).

\textbf{C. What is the worst-case complexity of the \textbf{Retrieve(\( S \), \( i \))} operation under this implementation? (Note that the operation should return the \( i \)-th non-deleted element.) You may use \( d \) to indicate the complexity.}

\end{document}
```

