We are constructing a hash table of size $m$ using the open addressing method (i.e., all elements are stored directly in the hash table without using pointers). A completely random hash function is used of the form $h(k,i)$, where $k$ is the key to be inserted into the table and $i$ is the attempt number. (This is the uniform probing method. The value returned by calling $h(k,i)$ is an integer drawn uniformly from 2 to $(m-1)$, independent of the draws in previous calls). The table already contains $n$ distinct elements. We now perform a search for an element that is not present in the table. What is the expected number of table cells that are checked during the search? Provide as tight an answer as possible (non-asymptotic).

\end{document}

